---
import FlowShot from '../FlowShot.astro';
import FlowDetail from '../FlowDetail.astro';
import FlowShot from '../../../components/flow/FlowShot.astro';
import FlowDetail from '../../../components/flow/FlowDetail.astro';
import shotLongDocs from '../../../assets/flow/shots/flow-long-documents-shot.webp';
import shotLongDocsMenu from '../../../assets/flow/shots/flow-long-documents-menu-shot.webp';
import detailParts from '../../../assets/flow/details/detail-parts.webp';
---
<!-- Chapter 2: Long documents -->
<section class="border-t border-border/60 bg-surface-raised/30 px-6 py-14 sm:py-20" aria-labelledby="tour-longdocs">
  {/* No max-w-6xl wrapper: the stage below runs wider than the copy on
      purpose, and a 6xl parent would clamp it back to the old width. */}
  <div>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl text-center">
      <p class="of-eyebrow" data-animate="rise">Long documents</p>
      <h3 id="tour-longdocs" class="of-display scroll-mt-28 mt-4 text-[clamp(1.85rem,3.4vw,2.75rem)]" data-animate="lead">Your 50-page document is not too big. It is four reviews.</h3>
      <p class="of-lede mx-auto mt-5 text-left" data-animate="rise">
        Most AI tools refuse a long document, or quietly cut it and answer about the piece that fit.
        Flow does neither. It plans the document as ordered parts that follow your document's own
        structure. Each part gets its own review, its own evidence, and its own receipt. The finish
        line reports coverage in counts a human can check: "You approved 3 of 4 parts. Everything
        else stays exactly as written."
      </p>
    </div>
    {/* The coverage line is this chapter's whole claim, so it rides over
        the review window as the stage crop rather than sitting in its own
        band above it. Bottom-right here; the neighbouring chapters take
        other corners (see .of-stage in global.css). */}
    <div class="of-stage of-stage--pad-bottom mt-12" data-animate="plate">
      <FlowShot
        src={shotLongDocs}
        alt="A live two-part review on a 3,905-word document in Flow: part chips, an honest coverage line, part one's own range highlighted, and Skip as a first-class choice."
        sizes="(min-width: 1280px) 78rem, (min-width: 1024px) 64rem, 100vw"
      />
      <FlowDetail
        class="of-stage__detail of-stage__detail--br"
        src={detailParts}
        alt="Flow's review header on a long document: numbered part chips, and the line '0 approved, 0 declined, 2 remaining'."
        caption="Coverage in plain counts · Approve, decline, or skip parts in any order"
        sizes="(min-width: 1280px) 30rem, (min-width: 1024px) 27rem, calc(100vw - 3rem)"
        glow
        animate
      />
    </div>
    {/* The disclosure menu is a separate claim — Flow tells you it will
        split BEFORE anything runs — so it keeps its own smaller frame
        rather than competing with the stage above. */}
    <div class="mx-auto mt-12 grid max-w-5xl items-center gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[0.92fr_1.08fr]">
      <FlowShot
        src={shotLongDocsMenu}
        alt="Flow's Agency menu disclosing up front: Improves this document in about 2 parts, and Too long to check at once, before anything runs."
        caption='"Improves this document in about 2 parts", before the menu closes.'
        crop
        sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 26rem, 100vw"
      />
      <div>
        <p class="text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed text-text-muted">
          A split never lands inside anything fragile. Boundaries fall only between top-level
          blocks, never inside code or front matter, and the parts reconstruct your document
          byte for byte. That is a tested invariant, not an aspiration.
        </p>
        <p class="of-spec-fine mt-5">
          Today this runs on local models. Cloud models still decline documents past one
          window, and Flow says so instead of pretending.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
